{"item": "B-0114", "job": "adjudicate-b-0114", "kind": "ruling", "product": "asf", "text": "What was disputed was one blocking finding: round 3's C1, that `tests/test_hermetic.py`'s `test_both_of_the_suites_entry_points_are_hermetic` pinned one hermetic guard and not both. It is upheld, because I reproduced it rather than reasoning about it: with `hermetic.strip_git_config(os.environ)` deleted from `tests/test_00_home.py:31`, the test was `OK` while the mode that twin exists for — `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_00_home.py`, which loads the module top-level and never runs `tests/__init__.py` — was `FAILED (failures=1)` on `'core.hookspath' unexpectedly found`. A test whose comment claims \"neither twin can be left behind again\" while staying green with a twin broken is worse than no test, so the exact edit was a third child run of that discover mode in the same loop; it is committed at `02bc263` and the test now goes red when *either* guard is removed (verified both ways). Round 3's I1 was non-blocking but factually true on inspection — `GhHost` appears nowhere in the tree and `squash_subject` runs before `host.merge`, not after — so it is closed too at `4c6d162`, docstring only. Everything else is overruled and does not return in any later round. Round 2's I2 (the hermeticity fix belongs on its own card) is overruled as a blocker: the code is correct and this branch's gate cannot run without it, so the carve-out is a record action, not a branch change, and it is a NEEDS OPERATOR line above. Round 3's I4 and I8 (`tests/test_dry_run.py:76` asserting byte-equality over `asf/harvest/harvest.py:746`'s `{time.monotonic() - started:.0f}`, and `tools/run_tests.py` silently returning a smaller denominator under load) are overruled as against this branch — I confirmed the branch touches none of those three paths, and I saw the same flake twice and then a clean `OK` on the identical head. Round 3's I5 is an environment defect, not a branch defect. Round 3's I2, I3, I6 and I7 are overruled as grounds for a further round: I verified I7's anchors myself (`(\"spec\", \"plan\")` does remain literal at `asf/evidence/evidence.py:502` and `:513`, and at `asf/harvest/lane.py:264` and `:619`), and they are a refactor of rows the card's *Expected* never names, on a branch already three rounds deep. What now holds is the card's contract, checked against the checkout and not against a review: both halves of *Expected* are implemented, and the *Acceptance* clause is `tests/test_doc_lane_landing.py:245` clause for clause — `(\"Active\", \"plan-approved\")` at `:251`, `[\"New\", \"New\"]` at `:253`, `rows.PLAN_CODE` per minted Task at `:259`. What changes because of it is that the review loop is closed at round 3 and the branch is landable, and that the card's empty footprint — the thing that let an unrelated hermeticity defect land here with nobody able to call it out of scope — is corrected in the `writes:` line below.", "ts": "2026-09-25T10:35:46Z"}
